# Line-ending policy for mcp-mayhem.
#
# ⛔ WHY THIS EXISTS. `core.autocrlf=true` on this machine and NO .gitattributes here meant
# every checkout rewrote tracked text to CRLF in the WORKING TREE, while git normalised it
# back to LF on commit. So the repo was always clean and the files on disk were not — and it
# is the files on disk that tools read.
#
# Measured 2026-09-17, two in one day, both from `pathlib.write_text` on Windows:
#     gitRobot/config/admission.v1.json                          87 CRLF
#     structuredJsonValidator/consumers/claims/claim.schema.json 103 CRLF (since 2026-09-15)
# verdictLedger's `test_no_tracked_json_config_carries_CRLF` caught both. Its own docstring
# records why that control exists: a CRLF config written from here once landed in ZeroParadox,
# whose `.gitattributes` declares `* text=auto eol=lf`, and their byte-portability check
# FAILED on it — a hard block on their push, arriving inside a change meant to help them.
#
# ⚠ The control is a DETECTOR and this is the FIX: without it the next checkout re-introduces
# CRLF and the suite reports a condition nobody's edit caused. Same argument as everywhere
# else here — make the defect unrepresentable rather than merely detected.
#
# ⚠ AND IT MATTERS MORE THAN TIDINESS ACROSS THE BOUNDARY: verdict identity in this fleet is
# content-keyed on git blob ids. Line endings are bytes, so a file whose endings differ by
# machine is a different subject to the ledger, and every verdict about it reads STALE.

# Default: auto-detect text, LF in the working tree and in the repo.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Source and config — explicit LF
*.py            text eol=lf
*.json          text eol=lf
*.jsonl         text eol=lf
*.md            text eol=lf
*.ps1           text eol=lf
*.psm1          text eol=lf
*.psd1          text eol=lf
*.toml          text eol=lf
*.cfg           text eol=lf
*.ini           text eol=lf
*.txt           text eol=lf
*.yml           text eol=lf
*.yaml          text eol=lf
.gitignore      text eol=lf
.gitattributes  text eol=lf

# Binary — never normalise
*.pdf   binary
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.zip   binary
*.olean binary
